{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2494 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7499702d55075b8ff4abd89f50418ad615874f18379cd754707d9d38bdbab4e4" :edb/db-before-t 2493 :edb/db-after-t 2494 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535806 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:43:06.864Z" 13194139535806 true] [17592186164948 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164951 13194139535806 true] [17592186166507 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/assets/73b3d7951ab2c2a355763f71d2dd97dc4c9c5921bc087ae83af3936b0daf5d8a.png)\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" 13194139535806 true] [17592186166507 1100 "${-1,\\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ [- \\frac{π}{2}, 0] \\\\ 2,\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ (0, \\frac{π}{2})$\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." 13194139535806 false] [17592186166507 1145 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x) = \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x$, then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n$S(x) = f(x)$\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x$, then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n$S(x) = \\dfrac{f^-(x)+f^+(x)}{2}$\nwhere $f^-(x)$ and $f^-(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nThe one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-}(\\frac{π}{2}) &= lim_(x → (π/2)^{-})f(x) = 2 \\\\\nf^{+}(\\frac{π}{2}) &= lim_(x → (π/2)^{+})f(x) =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f^-\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+f^+\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}{2} = \\dfrac{2+(-1)}{2} = \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$" 13194139535806 false] [17592186166507 1145 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x) = \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x) = f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x) = \\dfrac{f^-(x)+f^+(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $f^-(x)$ and $f^-(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{-}}f(x)=2 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f^-\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+f^+\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}{2} = \\dfrac{2+(-1)}{2} = \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535806 true] [17592186166508 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/assets/1da7fe97b22b7a72b4253abfb180ce5f294fa945450879d8cb857ee1470edbf3.png)\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nUse this Fourier series to find the sum of the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}.\n$$" 13194139535806 true] [17592186166508 1100 "${-1,\\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ [- π, 0] \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ (0, π)$\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n$f(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin((2n-1)x)$.\nUse this Fourier series to find the sum of the series\n$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}$." 13194139535806 false] [17592186166508 1145 "First, we notice that at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left((2n-1)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi\\right) = (-1)^{n+1}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the given Fourier series gives\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin\\left((2n-1)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)}(-1)^{n+1}\n$$\n\nNext, since $f(x)$ is continuous at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nthe Fourier series converges to\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince $[MATH: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in [0,\\pi),]$ we have\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(4)/(π(2n - 1))(-1)^{n + 1} \\\\\n\\frac{π}{4} &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n + 1})/(2n - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} = \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}}\n$$" 13194139535806 false] [17592186166508 1145 "First, we notice that at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left((2n-1)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi\\right) = (-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ into the given Fourier series gives\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin\\left((2n-1)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n= \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)}(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, since $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ the Fourier series converges to $f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in [0,\\pi),$ we have\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\pi (2n-1)}(-1)^{n+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} = \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}}.\n$$" 13194139535806 true] [17592186166892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367306 13194139535806 true] [17592186166892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367310 13194139535806 true] [17592186166892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367313 13194139535806 true] [17592186166892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367316 13194139535806 true] [17592186166892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367320 13194139535806 true] [17592186166893 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367323 13194139535806 true] [17592186166893 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367341 13194139535806 true] [17592186166893 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367359 13194139535806 true] [17592186166893 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367377 13194139535806 true] [17592186167232 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367294 13194139535806 true] [17592186167232 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367297 13194139535806 true] [17592186167232 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367301 13194139535806 true] [17592186167232 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535806 true] [17592186167232 1100 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(